eject budgets below the quota sum or above total capacity.\" So we should raise ValueError or return special? Since function must return JSON-serializable object, we can return an error object. But better to raise exception? The spec says \"must return a JSON-serializable object\", but also \"validate every required field before computing a result.\" It doesn't specify how to handle invalid budget. I'll assume we should return an object with an \"error\" field. However the task says \"Reject budgets ...\" meaning the implementation should not proceed. I'll return a dict with \"error\": \"budget out of range\". But to be safe, we can raise ValueError, but the function signature says must return JSON-serializable. I'll return an error dict.\n\nAlgorithm: start allocated = {seg.name: seg.minimum for seg in segments}. remaining = budget - sum_min. While remaining > 0: for each segment, compute metric = importance / (allocated[seg.name] + 1). Pick segment with max metric, tie by original order. Increase allocated by 1, remaining--. Return result.\n\nNote: The segments list order is important for tie-breaking. We'll keep original indices.\n\nImplementation: parse case['payload'] to get segments list and budget. Use list of dicts with name, importance, minimum, capacity. Use enumerate for tie-break.\n\nReturn dict with segments: list of dicts {\"name\": name, \"allocated\": allocated[name]}, and \"total_allocated\": budget (conserved total). Also maybe \"name_mapping\"? Not sure. We'll just return that.\n\nCode: def solve(case): payload = case['payload']; segments = payload['segments']; budget = payload['budget']; ... etc.\n\nWrite Python source as string, escape for JSON.\n\nI'll produce the JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as string of Python code.\n\nResponse must be exactly one JSON object with single key \"implementation\". No extra text. So I'll output: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"} escaped properly.\n\nLet's write the python c